> 
> In the meantime, I had 2 questions:
> 
> a) I superficially understand the basic difference between Rserve and RJava (Rserve is a client-server interface while RJava is a JNI based solution) and that Rserve allows one to maintain multiple R sessions. I would be grateful if someone could expand on the advantages of JRI (RJava) over Rserve, if any.
> 

rJava supports references since it embeds R into your Java application and also REPL callbacks. This is usually useful to R GUIs (such as JGR). The disadvantage is that due to the lack of separation if R crashes so does your Java application and vice versa. Also the setup can be more daunting than Rserve as it involves shared libraries. In addition, since R is single-threaded you can only have one synchronous R thread in your Java application and there is no way to re-start R.


> Also, in a single-user, single-computer (multi-core) scenario, is there any advantage to being able to call multiple R sessions ?
> 

In general R is single threaded so using multiple R instances is useful for parallel computing (although there are now other options such as the multicore package).



